1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,430
A command has three channels: one for input,

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,160
one for normal output and one for errors.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,625
Once you control them,

4
00:00:07,625 --> 00:00:08,956
you can save output,

5
00:00:08,956 --> 00:00:11,487
hide noise and join commands together.

6
00:00:12,037 --> 00:00:13,907
Today you learn four things.

7
00:00:13,907 --> 00:00:15,711
The three standard streams.

8
00:00:15,711 --> 00:00:16,512
Redirection,

9
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:17,982
to send them to files.

10
00:00:17,982 --> 00:00:18,383
Pipes,

11
00:00:18,383 --> 00:00:19,519
to join commands.

12
00:00:19,519 --> 00:00:21,189
And safe temporary files.

13
00:00:21,739 --> 00:00:24,533
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

14
00:00:26,583 --> 00:00:27,005
First,

15
00:00:27,005 --> 00:00:28,272
the three streams.

16
00:00:28,272 --> 00:00:30,383
Every command has standard in,

17
00:00:30,383 --> 00:00:31,368
numbered zero.

18
00:00:31,368 --> 00:00:32,283
Standard out,

19
00:00:32,283 --> 00:00:33,198
numbered one,

20
00:00:33,198 --> 00:00:34,464
for normal output.

21
00:00:34,464 --> 00:00:35,801
And standard error,

22
00:00:35,801 --> 00:00:36,716
numbered two,

23
00:00:36,716 --> 00:00:37,490
for errors.

24
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:38,264
Watch both.

25
00:00:38,264 --> 00:00:40,094
List one file that exists,

26
00:00:40,094 --> 00:00:41,642
and one that does not.

27
00:00:41,642 --> 00:00:42,345
Type: L S,

28
00:00:42,345 --> 00:00:44,245
slash E T C slash hostname,

29
00:00:44,245 --> 00:00:44,668
space,

30
00:00:44,668 --> 00:00:45,442
slash nope.

31
00:00:45,992 --> 00:00:47,632
Two lines on the screen.

32
00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:48,316
The error,

33
00:00:48,316 --> 00:00:49,546
and the file name.

34
00:00:49,546 --> 00:00:50,845
They look the same,

35
00:00:50,845 --> 00:00:53,374
but they travel on different streams.

36
00:00:53,374 --> 00:00:57,270
Now send standard out to a file with a greater than sign.

37
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:57,954
Type: L S,

38
00:00:57,954 --> 00:00:59,799
slash E T C slash hostname,

39
00:00:59,799 --> 00:01:00,209
space,

40
00:01:00,209 --> 00:01:00,961
slash nope,

41
00:01:00,961 --> 00:01:01,850
greater than,

42
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:02,738
out dot text.

43
00:01:03,288 --> 00:01:05,804
Only the error is left on the screen.

44
00:01:05,804 --> 00:01:08,320
The normal output went into the file.

45
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:09,340
Look inside it.

46
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:10,904
Type: cat out dot text.

47
00:01:11,454 --> 00:01:12,476
The file name,

48
00:01:12,476 --> 00:01:13,424
and no error.

49
00:01:13,424 --> 00:01:14,883
Now do the opposite.

50
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:15,175
Two,

51
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:16,124
greater than,

52
00:01:16,124 --> 00:01:18,093
sends the errors to a file.

53
00:01:18,093 --> 00:01:18,823
Type: L S,

54
00:01:18,823 --> 00:01:20,793
slash E T C slash hostname,

55
00:01:20,793 --> 00:01:21,231
space,

56
00:01:21,231 --> 00:01:22,033
slash nope,

57
00:01:22,033 --> 00:01:22,471
space,

58
00:01:22,471 --> 00:01:23,711
two greater than,

59
00:01:23,711 --> 00:01:24,660
err dot text.

60
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:26,746
Now the file name shows,

61
00:01:26,746 --> 00:01:28,154
and the error is gone.

62
00:01:28,154 --> 00:01:29,434
Check where it went.

63
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:30,906
Type: cat err dot text.

64
00:01:31,456 --> 00:01:32,732
The error message,

65
00:01:32,732 --> 00:01:33,158
saved.

66
00:01:33,158 --> 00:01:34,931
Stream one holds results.

67
00:01:34,931 --> 00:01:36,775
Stream two holds problems.

68
00:01:36,775 --> 00:01:37,910
Keep them apart,

69
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:39,754
and each is easier to use.

70
00:01:41,804 --> 00:01:42,168
Next,

71
00:01:42,168 --> 00:01:43,405
more redirection.

72
00:01:43,405 --> 00:01:46,244
A single greater than creates the file,

73
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:47,336
or replaces it.

74
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:48,573
Type: echo first,

75
00:01:48,573 --> 00:01:49,519
greater than,

76
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:50,465
log dot text.

77
00:01:51,015 --> 00:01:51,465
Silent,

78
00:01:51,465 --> 00:01:53,907
because the output went into the file.

79
00:01:53,907 --> 00:01:56,092
Now two greater than signs append,

80
00:01:56,092 --> 00:01:58,084
and keep what is already there.

81
00:01:58,084 --> 00:01:59,241
Type: echo second,

82
00:01:59,241 --> 00:02:00,527
double greater than,

83
00:02:00,527 --> 00:02:01,362
log dot text.

84
00:02:01,912 --> 00:02:04,792
Look at the file. Type: cat log dot text.

85
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:06,105
Both lines.

86
00:02:06,105 --> 00:02:09,852
A single greater than would have erased the first one,

87
00:02:09,852 --> 00:02:11,378
so be careful with it.

88
00:02:11,378 --> 00:02:16,582
A less than sign does the reverse: it feeds a file into a command as input.

89
00:02:16,582 --> 00:02:17,692
Count the lines.

90
00:02:17,692 --> 00:02:18,386
Type: W C,

91
00:02:18,386 --> 00:02:18,872
dash l,

92
00:02:18,872 --> 00:02:19,565
less than,

93
00:02:19,565 --> 00:02:20,467
log dot text.

94
00:02:21,017 --> 00:02:21,702
Two lines.

95
00:02:21,702 --> 00:02:21,975
Now,

96
00:02:21,975 --> 00:02:27,382
a special file named slash dev slash null throws away everything written to it.

97
00:02:27,382 --> 00:02:28,409
Hide the error.

98
00:02:28,409 --> 00:02:29,093
Type: L S,

99
00:02:29,093 --> 00:02:29,846
slash nope,

100
00:02:29,846 --> 00:02:30,257
space,

101
00:02:30,257 --> 00:02:31,420
two greater than,

102
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:32,857
slash dev slash null.

103
00:02:33,407 --> 00:02:34,444
Nothing at all.

104
00:02:34,444 --> 00:02:36,379
But did the command succeed?

105
00:02:36,379 --> 00:02:38,867
Every command leaves an exit status,

106
00:02:38,867 --> 00:02:41,424
in the variable dollar question mark.

107
00:02:41,424 --> 00:02:42,737
Zero means success.

108
00:02:42,737 --> 00:02:43,497
Type: echo,

109
00:02:43,497 --> 00:02:44,949
dollar question mark.

110
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:49,147
Two, so it failed. Hiding the message did not hide the failure.

111
00:02:51,197 --> 00:02:51,848
Now pipes.

112
00:02:51,848 --> 00:02:56,929
A pipe sends the standard out of one command into the standard in of the next.

113
00:02:56,929 --> 00:02:58,361
Here is the log again.

114
00:02:58,361 --> 00:02:59,794
Type: cat app dot log.

115
00:03:00,344 --> 00:03:01,068
Six lines.

116
00:03:01,068 --> 00:03:02,733
Send them through grep,

117
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:04,470
to keep only the errors.

118
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:06,062
Type: cat app dot log,

119
00:03:06,062 --> 00:03:06,496
space,

120
00:03:06,496 --> 00:03:06,858
pipe,

121
00:03:06,858 --> 00:03:07,292
space,

122
00:03:07,292 --> 00:03:08,088
grep ERROR.

123
00:03:08,638 --> 00:03:09,440
Two lines.

124
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:10,883
Add another stage,

125
00:03:10,883 --> 00:03:12,005
to count them.

126
00:03:12,005 --> 00:03:14,330
Type: grep ERROR app dot log,

127
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:14,811
space,

128
00:03:14,811 --> 00:03:15,212
pipe,

129
00:03:15,212 --> 00:03:15,693
space,

130
00:03:15,693 --> 00:03:16,013
W C,

131
00:03:16,013 --> 00:03:16,574
dash l.

132
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:17,922
Two errors.

133
00:03:17,922 --> 00:03:21,838
Now suppose you want to see the data and also save it.

134
00:03:21,838 --> 00:03:24,738
The tee command writes a copy to a file,

135
00:03:24,738 --> 00:03:26,406
and passes the data on.

136
00:03:26,406 --> 00:03:28,509
Type: grep ERROR app dot log,

137
00:03:28,509 --> 00:03:28,871
pipe,

138
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:30,321
tee errors dot text,

139
00:03:30,321 --> 00:03:30,684
pipe,

140
00:03:30,684 --> 00:03:30,974
W C,

141
00:03:30,974 --> 00:03:31,482
dash l.

142
00:03:32,032 --> 00:03:33,171
The count came out,

143
00:03:33,171 --> 00:03:35,330
and the file was written on the way.

144
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:35,870
Check it.

145
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:37,429
Type: cat errors dot text.

146
00:03:37,979 --> 00:03:39,283
The two error lines,

147
00:03:39,283 --> 00:03:39,675
saved.

148
00:03:39,675 --> 00:03:40,261
Remember,

149
00:03:40,261 --> 00:03:42,414
a pipe carries standard out only.

150
00:03:42,414 --> 00:03:44,370
Errors still go to the screen,

151
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:46,000
unless you redirect them.

152
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:48,395
Last,

153
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:49,499
temporary files.

154
00:03:49,499 --> 00:03:51,984
A script often needs a scratch file.

155
00:03:51,984 --> 00:03:53,778
Never invent a fixed name,

156
00:03:53,778 --> 00:03:55,573
because it is predictable,

157
00:03:55,573 --> 00:03:58,264
and other users can plant a link there.

158
00:03:58,264 --> 00:04:00,542
Ask for a safe one with M K temp.

159
00:04:00,542 --> 00:04:02,060
Type: f equals dollar,

160
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:03,371
open round bracket,

161
00:04:03,371 --> 00:04:03,992
M K temp,

162
00:04:03,992 --> 00:04:05,373
close round bracket.

163
00:04:05,923 --> 00:04:06,393
Silent,

164
00:04:06,393 --> 00:04:09,216
because the name went into the variable f.

165
00:04:09,216 --> 00:04:09,956
Look at it.

166
00:04:09,956 --> 00:04:10,695
Type: echo,

167
00:04:10,695 --> 00:04:11,569
double quote,

168
00:04:11,569 --> 00:04:12,174
dollar f,

169
00:04:12,174 --> 00:04:13,048
double quote.

170
00:04:13,598 --> 00:04:15,206
A new file in slash temp,

171
00:04:15,206 --> 00:04:17,843
with a random name that nobody can guess,

172
00:04:17,843 --> 00:04:19,966
created with private permissions.

173
00:04:19,966 --> 00:04:21,573
Write the errors into it.

174
00:04:21,573 --> 00:04:23,439
Type: grep ERROR app dot log,

175
00:04:23,439 --> 00:04:24,275
greater than,

176
00:04:24,275 --> 00:04:25,111
double quote,

177
00:04:25,111 --> 00:04:25,690
dollar f,

178
00:04:25,690 --> 00:04:26,526
double quote.

179
00:04:27,076 --> 00:04:32,900
Count its lines. Type: W C, dash l, less than, double quote, dollar f, double quote.

180
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:34,115
Two lines.

181
00:04:34,115 --> 00:04:35,579
When you are finished,

182
00:04:35,579 --> 00:04:36,643
always clean up.

183
00:04:36,643 --> 00:04:37,308
Type: R M,

184
00:04:37,308 --> 00:04:38,173
double quote,

185
00:04:38,173 --> 00:04:38,772
dollar f,

186
00:04:38,772 --> 00:04:39,637
double quote.

187
00:04:40,187 --> 00:04:44,560
Check that it is gone. Type: L S, double quote, dollar f, double quote.

188
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:45,980
No such file.

189
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:46,315
Make,

190
00:04:46,315 --> 00:04:46,582
use,

191
00:04:46,582 --> 00:04:47,051
remove.

192
00:04:47,051 --> 00:04:48,389
In the next lessons,

193
00:04:48,389 --> 00:04:51,937
a trap will do that cleanup even when a script fails.

194
00:04:53,987 --> 00:04:56,592
Now a small job that uses everything.

195
00:04:56,592 --> 00:04:58,070
Count the log levels,

196
00:04:58,070 --> 00:04:59,197
save the report,

197
00:04:59,197 --> 00:05:01,168
and keep the problems apart.

198
00:05:01,168 --> 00:05:02,294
Here is the log.

199
00:05:02,294 --> 00:05:03,843
Type: cat app dot log.

200
00:05:04,393 --> 00:05:05,579
Count the levels,

201
00:05:05,579 --> 00:05:07,673
and send the report to a file.

202
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:08,370
Type: awk,

203
00:05:08,370 --> 00:05:09,277
single quote,

204
00:05:09,277 --> 00:05:10,045
open brace,

205
00:05:10,045 --> 00:05:11,371
print dollar three,

206
00:05:11,371 --> 00:05:12,208
close brace,

207
00:05:12,208 --> 00:05:13,115
single quote,

208
00:05:13,115 --> 00:05:13,953
app dot log,

209
00:05:13,953 --> 00:05:14,301
pipe,

210
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:14,650
sort,

211
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:14,999
pipe,

212
00:05:14,999 --> 00:05:15,976
unique dash c,

213
00:05:15,976 --> 00:05:16,883
greater than,

214
00:05:16,883 --> 00:05:17,790
out dot text.

215
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:23,631
No output, because it went to the file. Look at the report. Type: cat out dot text.

216
00:05:24,181 --> 00:05:24,934
Two errors,

217
00:05:24,934 --> 00:05:27,193
three info lines and one warning.

218
00:05:27,193 --> 00:05:30,343
Now run grep on a good file and a missing one,

219
00:05:30,343 --> 00:05:32,945
and keep the errors in their own file.

220
00:05:32,945 --> 00:05:33,698
Type: grep,

221
00:05:33,698 --> 00:05:34,177
dash c,

222
00:05:34,177 --> 00:05:34,588
ERROR,

223
00:05:34,588 --> 00:05:35,409
app dot log,

224
00:05:35,409 --> 00:05:36,505
missing dot log,

225
00:05:36,505 --> 00:05:37,669
two greater than,

226
00:05:37,669 --> 00:05:38,901
problems dot text.

227
00:05:39,451 --> 00:05:41,222
The count for the good file.

228
00:05:41,222 --> 00:05:44,511
The complaint about the missing file went elsewhere.

229
00:05:44,511 --> 00:05:44,827
Look.

230
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:46,598
Type: cat problems dot text.

231
00:05:47,148 --> 00:05:51,286
Results on the screen, problems in a file. That is a script you can trust.

232
00:05:53,336 --> 00:05:54,659
Four myths to clear up.

233
00:05:55,209 --> 00:05:57,750
Myth one: greater than adds to a file.

234
00:05:57,750 --> 00:05:57,951
No.

235
00:05:57,951 --> 00:05:59,356
It replaces the file.

236
00:05:59,356 --> 00:05:59,824
To add,

237
00:05:59,824 --> 00:06:01,630
use two greater than signs.

238
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:04,508
Myth two: a pipe carries errors too.

239
00:06:04,508 --> 00:06:04,702
No.

240
00:06:04,702 --> 00:06:06,577
It carries standard out only.

241
00:06:06,577 --> 00:06:08,452
Errors need two greater than.

242
00:06:09,002 --> 00:06:14,037
Myth three: slash dev slash null stores output. No. It throws everything away.

243
00:06:14,587 --> 00:06:20,261
Myth four: a fixed name in slash temp is fine. No. It is predictable. Use M K temp.

244
00:06:20,811 --> 00:06:23,862
Streams. Redirects. Pipes. Safe temp files.

245
00:06:25,912 --> 00:06:30,477
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

246
00:06:31,027 --> 00:06:34,867
Name the three standard streams, and their numbers: zero, one and two.

247
00:06:35,417 --> 00:06:40,047
Redirect output with greater than and double greater than, and input with less than.

248
00:06:40,597 --> 00:06:45,503
Send errors to a file with two greater than, or discard them with slash dev slash null.

249
00:06:46,053 --> 00:06:49,061
Join commands with a pipe, and save a copy with tee.

250
00:06:49,611 --> 00:06:53,387
Create a temporary file with M K temp, and remove it when done.

251
00:06:53,937 --> 00:06:56,817
Read the exit status, with dollar question mark.

252
00:06:57,367 --> 00:06:58,989
To pass, you need all six.

253
00:07:01,039 --> 00:07:05,839
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Type every command yourself.

254
00:07:06,389 --> 00:07:11,680
One: list one file that exists and one that does not, and see both streams on the screen.

255
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:14,891
Two: write a line to a file with greater than,

256
00:07:14,891 --> 00:07:16,800
add one with double greater than,

257
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:18,246
and count with less than.

258
00:07:18,796 --> 00:07:21,907
Three: send errors to a file with two greater than,

259
00:07:21,907 --> 00:07:24,591
then discard them with slash dev slash null,

260
00:07:24,591 --> 00:07:26,177
and print the exit status.

261
00:07:26,727 --> 00:07:30,226
Four: build a pipeline with tee, and check the saved copy.

262
00:07:30,776 --> 00:07:36,024
Five: make a temporary file with M K temp, write to it, read it back and remove it.

263
00:07:36,574 --> 00:07:40,051
Six: save a report to one file, and the errors to another.

264
00:07:40,601 --> 00:07:43,908
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

265
00:07:59,458 --> 00:08:00,333
Quick check.

266
00:08:00,883 --> 00:08:02,483
Which number is standard error?

267
00:08:03,033 --> 00:08:06,318
What is the difference between greater than, and double greater than?

268
00:08:06,868 --> 00:08:08,340
Does a pipe carry errors?

269
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:11,173
Which command makes a safe temporary file?

270
00:08:11,723 --> 00:08:17,419
Objective covered: MC05-O05.

271
00:08:17,969 --> 00:08:20,038
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

272
00:08:30,588 --> 00:08:34,300
Next lesson: error handling, traps, logging and exit codes.

273
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:38,178
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
